In a previous article, daly@ecs.umass.edu (Bryon Daly, ECE dept, UMass, Amherst) says:

>While perusing the monster attributes list the other day, I noticed two
>unique monsters that don't seemed to be used in the game:
>
>Charon, a 76th level peaceful monster
>Ceberus, a 12th level dog in hell
>
>Both are included in #ifdef CHARON statements, making it appear that CHARON
>is some sort of unused option if the game.  I couldn't find any other use
>of CHARON anywhere in the source.  Does anyone know what the plans are/were
>for this stuff?  It seems like there are all sorts of interesting 
>possibilities...a river Styx that you must pay Charon (through the nose) to 
>cross...a "Gates of Hell" level beneath the castle, guarded by Cerberus...etc.
>

	The "I don't know the answer to your question but have something
to add" department: from the RUMORS file, containing fortune cookie
fortunes and cheapo Oracle info:

"They say that you can only cross the River Styx in Charon's boat."
"Row (3x) that boat gently down the stream, Charon (4x), death is but 
a dream."

	That first one could imply that someone, somewhere, was thinking
about doing something special with Charon, but then again, maybe not.
The fortune cookie fortunes are often useful, but are sometimes just
bizzareness thrown in for the hell of it ("I smell a maze of twisty
little passages"), sometimes intentionally misleading ("If you're lost,
try buying a map next time you are in a shop" or "Try calling your katana
Moulinette"), and sometimes outright lies ("They say that a cat avoids
traps" or "If you kill the wizard, you get promoted to demi-god"). I
like the idea of having some more special levels, though. It would
make the game more of an adventure, and less simple hack-and-slash.

 
